WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.190
On Earth, a flame has a shape we all know: a flickering teardrop.

2
00:00:05.689 --> 00:00:07.895
That shape comes from gravity.

3
00:00:08.324 --> 00:00:16.602
Gas heated by the flame is lighter than the air around it, so it rises, and cooler air rushes in underneath.

4
00:00:17.032 --> 00:00:21.827
The push on that hot gas is the difference in density, times gravity.

5
00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:23.650
Now take gravity away.

6
00:00:24.010 --> 00:00:30.058
On the space station, nothing is lighter or heavier. Hot gas stays where it is.

7
00:00:30.476 --> 00:00:38.963
Oxygen can only reach the flame by slowly drifting in, so the flame becomes a small, steady, dim blue sphere.

8
00:00:39.613 --> 00:00:44.234
Without fresh air arriving, a flame in orbit can even smother itself.

9
00:00:44.710 --> 00:00:50.643
The Moon and Mars sit in between, at one sixth and about three eighths of Earth's gravity.

10
00:00:51.211 --> 00:01:00.639
Every one of NASA's 1,276 fire tests in this database ran in microgravity. Nobody has measured a fire at Moon gravity yet.

11
00:01:01.336 --> 00:01:07.384
So for a Moon Base, the honest answer lies somewhere between space and Earth.
